## What happened

**.NET 11 dropped DSA support on macOS.** Apple's native cryptographic libraries (Security.framework / CommonCrypto) have never supported DSA. Earlier .NET versions used an OpenSSL-based fallback on macOS, but Microsoft removed that path — so `DSA.Create()` now throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS.

This follows the broader .NET deprecation trajectory for DSA (it was already partially restricted on Apple platforms in .NET 7+).

## How to fix

Since you only need to **verify** legacy signatures, you have two practical options:

### Option 1 – Use a managed DSA implementation (recommended)
Use **BouncyCastle**, which provides a pure-managed DSA that works on all platforms:

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

// Load your existing DSA public key parameters
var dsaParams = new DsaPublicKeyParameters(/* y, p, q, g */);
var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA");
verifier.Init(forSigning: false, dsaParams);
verifier.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
bool valid = verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
```

### Option 2 – Platform guard + migration
If you want to keep using `System.Security.Cryptography` on Windows/Linux and only need a short-term fix:

```csharp
if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    // verify as before
}
else
{
    // Use BouncyCastle or another managed fallback
}
```

### Long-term recommendation
**Migrate away from DSA entirely.** DSA is a legacy algorithm with limited key sizes. Re-sign artifacts with **RSA** or **ECDSA** when feasible, and keep the managed BouncyCastle verifier only for signatures you can't re-issue.
